<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Corrections to the Kenyan return definition shipped by l10n_ke 19.0.

    These records belong to l10n_ke; we write over them by external id. That is
    legitimate and survives an upgrade of this module, but it does NOT survive
    an upgrade of l10n_ke, which reloads its own data and puts the defects back
    without reloading ours.

    Because every one of these defects produces a plausible-looking number
    rather than an error, a silent reversion is the dangerous case. So the
    renderer re-checks all of them on every compute and refuses to produce a
    figure from a reverted definition - see CORRECTED_EXPRESSIONS and
    REMOVED_EXPRESSIONS in models/ke_tax_correction.py, which must be kept in
    step with this file.
-->
<odoo>

    <!--
        Box 16 - Non-Deductible Input VAT.

        Section 17 of the VAT Act apportions input tax on mixed supplies by the
        share of turnover that is taxable. The shipped formula was

            box_15.tax - (((box_1.tax + box_2.tax + box_3.base) / box_5.base) * box_15.tax)

        which puts the VAT *charged* at 16% and 8% into the numerator of a ratio
        whose denominator is turnover. For a trader with only standard-rated
        sales the ratio collapses from 1.0 to roughly 0.16, so about 84% of the
        mixed input-VAT pool is treated as non-deductible instead of none of it.
        That understates box 17 and overstates box 22 - a real overpayment,
        every period.

        The correct numerator is taxable turnover, which box 6 already computes
        as box_1.base + box_2.base + box_3.base, so the fix reuses it rather
        than restating it.

        There is deliberately no 90/10 de minimis here. Section 17(6) used to
        allow the whole of the mixed pool when taxable supplies exceeded 90% of
        total supplies and none of it below 10%; the Tax Laws (Amendment) Act,
        2024 abolished both thresholds with effect from 27 December 2024, so
        mixed input VAT is now claimed strictly in proportion to taxable
        supplies. Do not put the thresholds back for a 2025+ period.
    -->
    <record id="l10n_ke.tax_report_non_deductible_input_vat_tax_tag"
            model="account.report.expression">
        <field name="formula">box_15.tax - ((box_6.base / box_5.base) * box_15.tax)</field>
        <field name="subformula">ignore_zero_division</field>
    </record>

    <!--
        Box 26 - Net VAT Payable / Credit Carried Forward.

        The carry-forward is conditional on the period netting to a credit, and
        the threshold is written in a currency. l10n_ke shipped RON - the
        Romanian leu - which is the only occurrence of RON anywhere outside
        l10n_ro, and l10n_ro does not use it. Every other localisation names its
        own currency (EUR, CAD, BGN, NGN, RWF, TSH). Kenya's is KES.

        Because the threshold is zero the practical effect depends on how the
        currency resolves, which is precisely why it should not be left to
        chance on a filed return.
    -->
    <record id="l10n_ke.tax_report_net_vat_payable_or_credit_carried_forward_carryover"
            model="account.report.expression">
        <field name="subformula">if_below(KES(0))</field>
    </record>

    <!--
        Box 20 - Total Withholding VAT Credit.

        Box 20 is the 2% withheld from us by our customers, so it is a
        sales-side figure. Its tax column correctly reads the tag 'WH Sales',
        applied by the sales tax SWT2. Its base column read 'WH base', which
        only the purchase-side tax PWT2 ever applies - the opposite side of the
        ledger.

        There is nothing correct to point it at: SWT2's base repartition carries
        no tag at all, so no sales-side withholding base exists to sum. Rather
        than display the purchase-side figure under a sales-side heading, the
        expression is removed and the column renders empty.

        Only box_20.tax feeds box 22, so no filed figure changes either way.
        This is a presentation fix, and it is the right one until the
        withholding-VAT certificate register gives the base a real source.

        Note: uninstalling this module does not restore the expression;
        reinstalling l10n_ke does.
    -->
    <delete model="account.report.expression"
            id="l10n_ke.tax_report_total_withholding_vat_credit_base_tag"/>

</odoo>
